WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.930
Franklin Delano Roosevelt was the longest
serving President in US history, serving 12

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:06.629
year in office from 1933-1945.

00:00:06.629 --> 00:00:13.370
He steered the nation through its worst economic
crisis, only to be faced with the most horrific

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:14.970
war in history.

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:20.030
Though facing titanic physical challenges
of his own, he imbued America with the indomitable

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:25.070
fortitude and sense of morale that was required
to spur the nation to victory.

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:31.310
In today’s Biographics we examine the inspirational
life of the 32nd President of the United States,

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:36.550
Franklin Delano Roosevelt.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:45.250
Early Years

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:50.370
Franklin Delano Roosevelt was born on January
30th, 1882, the only child of James and Sara

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.730
Ann Delano Roosevelt, in Hyde Park, New York.

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:56.580
James recorded the event in his diary that
day, writing . . .

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:01.550
At quarter to nine my Sallie had a splendid
large boy, but was unconscious when he was

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:02.550
born.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:04.670
Baby weighs ten pounds without clothes.

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:06.479
It wasn’t an easy birth.

00:01:06.479 --> 00:01:11.740
In fact, both mother and baby nearly died
due to the administration of too much chloroform

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:14.480
by the doctor.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:20.149
Despite being born not breathing, Franklin
went on to become a healthy child.

00:01:20.149 --> 00:01:24.539
His raised in a privileged family, living
in an impressive estate that had been in the

00:01:24.539 --> 00:01:26.280
Roosevelt family for more than a century.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:30.420
The Roosevelts were of Dutch extraction, having
emigrated in 1654.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:36.579
They became an Americans success story, due
largely to development in the coal and transportation

00:01:36.579 --> 00:01:37.579
industries.

00:01:37.579 --> 00:01:40.850
Franklin’s father, a Harvard Law School
graduate, served as the vice president of

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:46.399
the Delaware and Hudson Railway and also served
as president for the Southern Railway Security

00:01:46.399 --> 00:01:47.399
Company.

00:01:47.399 --> 00:01:50.539
His income and his inheritance allowed him
to retire to focus on his health, which had

00:01:50.539 --> 00:01:52.139
never been very good.

00:01:52.139 --> 00:01:54.350
Sara Delano was the second wife of James.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:56.789
Her existence revolved around caring for her
son.

00:01:56.789 --> 00:01:59.969
As a result, the majority of Franklin’s
early childhood was spent in the company of

00:01:59.969 --> 00:02:00.969
his mother.

00:02:00.969 --> 00:02:03.060
Sara was an overly protective parent.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:06.450
She came to feel resentful of anyone else
who got close to Franklin.

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:09.360
Meanwhile, James was often the absent father.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.220
Young Franklin benefited from the privileges
of wealth and position.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:16.780
When he was five years old, he visited the
White House with his father.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:19.660
President Grover Cleveland spoke to him personally,
saying . . .

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:24.520
I have just one wish for you, young man, that
you will never be President of the United

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:26.210
States.

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:31.290
A Privileged Childhood

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.480
Franklin didn’t attend school.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.140
Instead his father employed a private tutor
for him.

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.960
He was an able student who applied himself
to his lessons.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:43.650
Each summer, Franklin and Sara would vacation
at the Delano Homestead in Fairhaven, Massachusetts.

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:46.170
There would also be an annual trip to Europe.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:49.380
During these stays, Franklin learned to speak
German and French.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:52.070
As he grew older, Franklin became an avid
sportsman.

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:55.930
He played lawn tennis and polo, rode horses
and learned to shoot a rifle.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:58.400
In his teens he also took golf and sailing
lessons.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.320
It was customary for children of privilege
to attend boarding school at the age of twelve.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:07.200
But Sara considered that her glorious boy
was not yet ready to take such a big step

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.260
and delayed the move for two years.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:13.530
At fourteen, she enrolled him at the Groton
Episcopal School at Groton, Massachusetts.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:17.630
This school was attended by the children of
the most privileged.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:22.250
Although it was a shock for Franklin to suddenly
be away from the protective care of his mother.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:23.980
He soon adjusted to dormitory life.

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:27.490
He played a number of sports and became a
member of the school debating team as well

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:28.490
as the choir.

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:32.780
He graduated from the school in June, 1900
with grades sufficiently impressive to secure

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:36.880
a spot at Harvard University in Cambridge,
Massachusetts.

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.410
A Harvard Man
Franklin became very socially active during

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:40.510
his first year at Harvard.

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:44.540
He joined a number of clubs on campus and
began writing for the Harvard college newspaper.

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:49.570
He excelled in the college environment, becoming
a confident and popular student.

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:53.130
Towards the end of 1900, Franklin’s father
James died.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:55.910
The following year, another death profoundly
affected the nineteen him.

00:03:55.910 --> 00:04:00.060
President William McKinley was killed by an
assassin’s bullet, an act which propelled

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:03.680
Franklin’s distant cousin, Theodore Roosevelt
to the White House.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:06.940
This change of events served to pique Franklin’s
interest in his family history.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:11.960
He wrote a college paper titled ‘The Roosevelts
of New Amsterdam’ and studied the notations

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:15.740
in the old Dutch family Bible.

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:21.799
Eleanor Roosevelt
After three years at Harvard, Roosevelt was

00:04:21.799 --> 00:04:23.050
ready to graduate.

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:27.430
Yet he chose to stay on for another year in
order to work as the chief editor of the Harvard

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:28.610
Crimson newspaper.

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:32.740
One day in the summer of 1902, he was heading
back to the family mansion at Hyde Park from

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:40.349
New York when he happened upon eighteen-year-old
Eleanor Roosevelt, a distant relative.

00:04:40.349 --> 00:04:45.229
The two hit it off immediately and from that
chance encounter a romance blossomed.

00:04:45.229 --> 00:04:49.979
Franklin was captivated by Eleanor’s intellectual
depth and social conscience.

00:04:49.979 --> 00:04:54.490
Things got serious in 1903 with Franklin,
who was now studying law at Columbia University,

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:56.430
asking for Eleanor’s hand in marriage.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:05:00.220
She eagerly agreed and wanted the wedding
to take place as soon as possible.

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:04.720
Franklin’s mother, Sara, was not happy with
her son’s choice of bride.

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:10.050
Whether she had something specifically against
the independently minded Eleanor or felt threatened

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:14.889
by any woman getting between her and her beloved
boy is unclear.

00:05:14.889 --> 00:05:19.710
She convinced Franklin to at least delay the
wedding, which eventually took place on March

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:20.789
17, 1905.

00:05:20.789 --> 00:05:27.050
Eleanor, who was an orphan, was given away
by her uncle, President Theodore Roosevelt.

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:29.190
The young couple honeymooned in Europe.

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:32.860
Upon return to New York, Franklin returned
to his law studies at Columbia University.

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:37.900
In May, 1906, Eleanor gave birth to their
first child, who they named Anna Eleanor.

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:40.889
The following year Franklin passed the New
York State Bar exam.

00:05:40.889 --> 00:05:44.830
He secured his first job as a law clerk at
a prominent New York law firm.

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:48.279
A second child, James, came along in December,
1907.

00:05:48.279 --> 00:05:52.120
During 1907, Franklin’s mother, Sara had
been financing the building of a double house

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:54.680
on 65th Street, New York.

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:59.430
It was to be a present to the couple, with
her living in one house and them, in the other.

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:02.620
The living spaces were separated by a single
door.

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:07.219
While grateful, Eleanor was frustrated at
the way that Sara was smothering their marriage,

00:06:07.219 --> 00:06:10.189
making decisions that should have been Eleanor’s
domain.

00:06:10.189 --> 00:06:13.069
This was a difficult time in Eleanor’s life.

00:06:13.069 --> 00:06:17.400
Children were coming along regularly, yet
she admitted that she was not a ‘natural

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:18.400
mother’.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:21.009
She went so far as to write that she did not
like children.

00:06:21.009 --> 00:06:24.021
She hired a nanny and increasingly spent her
time on local New York social issues, such

00:06:24.021 --> 00:06:28.860
as working for improved factory conditions
for workers.

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:35.099
Politician
Franklin’s first foray into politics came

00:06:35.099 --> 00:06:39.060
in 1910, when he became a delegate to the
New York Democratic State Committee.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:42.650
He was then asked if he wanted to run for
the office of state assemblyman.

00:06:42.650 --> 00:06:45.879
It was assumed that the siting assemblyman
was about to retire.

00:06:45.879 --> 00:06:50.610
Franklin was enthused, but then found out
that the incumbent was not planning to retire

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:51.610
at all.

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:54.259
There was, though, an opening to run for New
York State senator.

00:06:54.259 --> 00:06:58.409
With no experience but plenty of enthusiasm,
he began a grass roots campaign that soon

00:06:58.409 --> 00:07:00.830
endeared him to the working people of the
city.

00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:03.629
He faced an uphill battle with the state being
decidedly Republican.

00:07:03.629 --> 00:07:06.909
However, on election day he found himself
ahead by more than a thousand votes.

00:07:06.909 --> 00:07:09.400
He was now the New York State Senator.

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:13.319
The Roosevelts now rented out their house
in New York City and moved to the state capitol

00:07:13.319 --> 00:07:14.839
in Albay, New York.

00:07:14.839 --> 00:07:18.050
Franklin soon became embroiled in political
intrigue.

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:23.090
A powerful faction of the New York Democratic
Party, known as Tammany Hall, had run the

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:24.449
party for decades.

00:07:24.449 --> 00:07:27.629
Roosevelt, however, was not one to be told
what to do.

00:07:27.629 --> 00:07:30.190
He was not going to be anybody’s puppet.

00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:34.080
He stated his opposition to the Tammany Hall
group and built his own support network who

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:37.870
were known in the papers as ‘the little
group of insurgents.’

00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:41.550
Through his adherence to principal and his
refusal to back down, Roosevelt developed

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:44.719
a reputation as man who would stand up for
his beliefs.

00:07:44.719 --> 00:07:48.610
As his first term in office drew to a close,
Roosevelt was struck down with typhoid fever

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:51.189
and, which has a 20% kill rate.

00:07:51.189 --> 00:07:55.539
He recovered in time to vigorously campaign
for a second term as New York state senator,

00:07:55.539 --> 00:08:01.349
which he won by 1,700 votes.

00:08:01.349 --> 00:08:07.219
Moving Up
Roosevelt was a supporter of Woodrow Wilson,

00:08:07.219 --> 00:08:09.490
who won the Presidency in 1912.

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:11.750
Wilson set about rewarding those who had stood
by him.

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:16.340
In due course Roosevelt was offered the position
of assistant secretary of the navy.

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:21.009
It was the same position that distant cousin
Theodore had held in 1897.

00:08:21.009 --> 00:08:24.750
Franklin now gave up his state senate seat
to take on the new role.

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:28.960
Roosevelt’s new job had him traveling the
country to inspect ships and naval yards.

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:33.919
He soon discovered that the navy was ill-prepared
for war and campaigned for the creation of

00:08:33.919 --> 00:08:38.820
a naval reserve of 50,00 men along with increased
spending on ship building.

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:42.620
Roosevelt didn’t seem to care that his words
or actions were not politically expedient.

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:45.220
His only interest was in bolstering the navy.

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:48.620
This attitude earned him respect across the
political spectrum.

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:53.709
With the outbreak of war in Europe in August,
1914, Roosevelt became even more dedicated

00:08:53.709 --> 00:08:56.029
to his work of building up the US Navy.

00:08:56.029 --> 00:08:59.509
He excelled in the role and won many advantages
for the navy.

00:08:59.509 --> 00:09:03.440
With the 1916 presidential election, Roosevelt’s
future hung in the balance.

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:07.470
If Wilson was returned to office, he would
no doubt retain his position as Assistant

00:09:07.470 --> 00:09:11.820
Navy Secretary, but if a Republican moved
into the White House, he would more than likely

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:13.990
be unceremoniously dumped.

00:09:13.990 --> 00:09:18.970
It was an extremely close fought race, with
Roosevelt later describing it as

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:21.220
“the most extraordinary day of my life.”

00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:26.810
At the end of the day, Wilson won out to retain
the Presidency and Roosevelt’s job was secure.

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:31.290
With America’s entry into the war in 1917,
his position took on more urgency.

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:35.870
He was instrumental in the drive to elicit
public donations for the war effort as well

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:39.160
as the development of mine detonation technology.

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:43.670
The pressures of his work kept Roosevelt away
from his family for increasingly long periods

00:09:43.670 --> 00:09:44.670
of time.

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:48.529
He began an affair with the social secretary
of his wife, Eleanor, that would continue,

00:09:48.529 --> 00:09:50.029
on and off, until his death.

00:09:50.029 --> 00:09:52.020
The woman’s name was Lucy Mercer.

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:55.270
Some time in 1918, Eleanor found letters between
the two.

00:09:55.270 --> 00:09:59.220
They revealed that Franklin was planning to
divorce her and make a new life with Lucy.

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:03.290
However, Franklin’s mother refused to permit
such a scandal, warning her son that it would

00:10:03.290 --> 00:10:04.779
ruin his political career.

00:10:04.779 --> 00:10:08.459
From that point on, the Roosevelt’s marriage
was one of expedience and convenience.

00:10:08.459 --> 00:10:12.459
Eleanor soon set up a separate home in Hyde
Park for her and the children and she threw

00:10:12.459 --> 00:10:14.070
herself into her social work.

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:17.839
The break in their marriage was so severe
that when Roosevelt asked Eleanor in 1942

00:10:17.839 --> 00:10:21.810
to come back and live with him, due to his
poor health, she refused

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:26.639
In September of 1918, Roosevelt made a state
visit to France and Britain to inspect naval

00:10:26.639 --> 00:10:27.639
facilities.

00:10:27.639 --> 00:10:32.740
On the return trip on board the USS Leviathan,
the influenza pandemic ran through the ship.

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:35.399
A number of people died and Roosevelt became
very ill.

00:10:35.399 --> 00:10:38.330
However, he recovered before reaching New
York.

00:10:38.330 --> 00:10:45.740
Vice Presidential Bid
Following the end of hostilities in 1918,

00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:48.660
Roosevelt began looking towards his next run
for office.

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:53.200
After much consultation he decided to campaign
for the Democratic vice-presidential nomination.

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:57.940
His bid was successful and he found himself
on the ticket alongside Ohio governor James

00:10:57.940 --> 00:11:00.140
Cox, who was running for president.

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:03.250
Roosevelt resigned as assistant secretary
of the navy and spent the next year and a

00:11:03.250 --> 00:11:04.250
half on the campaign trail.

00:11:04.250 --> 00:11:05.250
He championed Woodrow Wilson’s League of
Nations, which was widely unpopular.

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:09.560
The 1920 election ended up being a resounding
victory for the Republicans, with Warren G.

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:13.000
Harding securing the presidency.

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:19.360
Struck Down
Roosevelt accepted the loss with grace, returning

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:21.550
to New York to pick up his law practice.

00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:27.360
In August 1921, the family were vacationing
at Campobello Island when he was struck with

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:28.360
illness.

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:32.770
It was believed that he had contracted poliomyelitis,
with the condition living him paralyzed from

00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:33.770
the waist down.

00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:37.699
More recent analysis suggests that it may,
in fact, have been an autoimmune disease called

00:11:37.699 --> 00:11:39.480
Guillain–Barré syndrome.

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:43.290
Sara urged her son to retire from public life
and live out his life in retirement.

00:11:43.290 --> 00:11:47.310
But his closest advisers, including wife Eleanor,
felt otherwise.

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:51.940
He should continue his public career which,
they assured him, was on a trajectory toward

00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:52.940
the White House.

00:11:52.940 --> 00:11:55.910
Thus encouraged, he spent the next few years
in physical rehabilitation.

00:11:55.910 --> 00:12:00.279
If he couldn’t walk unaided, he would at
least project that impression to the public.

00:12:00.279 --> 00:12:04.190
He taught himself to walk a few paces with
metal braces on his hips and with the aid

00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:05.190
of a cane.

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:08.320
He was determined that the public would not
see him in a wheelchair.

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:13.750
In 1926, Roosevelt financed the building of
hydrotherapy debilitation clinic in Warm Springs,

00:12:13.750 --> 00:12:14.750
Georgia.

00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:17.639
He spent much of his time there throughout
the remainder of the 1920’s.

00:12:17.639 --> 00:12:20.980
During his rehab years, Roosevelt maintained
close contact with the Democratic Party.

00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:22.560
In 1925 he was appointed chairman of the Taconic
State Park Commission.

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:24.949
Then, in 1928, he reluctantly ran for Governor
of New York.

00:12:24.949 --> 00:12:27.509
He won by a margin of just one percent.

00:12:27.509 --> 00:12:31.670
His governorship was marked by a focus on
innovative infrastructure projects such as

00:12:31.670 --> 00:12:36.779
hydrostatic power plants and the institution
of his fireside chats during which he spoke

00:12:36.779 --> 00:12:39.160
directly over the radio to the people of New
York.

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:43.670
Winning reelection in 1930, Roosevelt introduced
an economic relief package to help the millions

00:12:43.670 --> 00:12:45.949
suffering from the effects of the depression.

00:12:45.949 --> 00:12:52.230
He also established a number of commissions
to root out public corruption.

00:12:52.230 --> 00:12:57.600
POTUS
As the popular two-term governor of the country’s

00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:02.690
most dynamic state, Roosevelt was a frontrunner
for the Democratic Presidential nomination

00:13:02.690 --> 00:13:03.850
in 1932.

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:06.190
He won the nomination at the Democratic National
Convention.

00:13:06.190 --> 00:13:10.829
In his acceptance speech he promised . . .
I pledge you, I pledge myself to a new deal

00:13:10.829 --> 00:13:12.000
for the American people...

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:14.110
This is more than a political campaign.

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:16.480
It is a call to arms.

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:20.780
Roosevelt ran on a platform of government
funded public works, farm relief and the repeal

00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:21.829
of prohibition.

00:13:21.829 --> 00:13:26.389
On the strength of these policies and his
winning personality, he won 57 percent of

00:13:26.389 --> 00:13:30.709
the popular vote to become the 32nd President
of the United States.

00:13:30.709 --> 00:13:34.370
After taking the oath of office, Roosevelt
delivered his inaugural address, best known

00:13:34.370 --> 00:13:44.629
for its famous first line:
So, first of all, let me assert my firm belief

00:13:44.629 --> 00:13:51.449
that the only thing we have to fear is...fear
itself — nameless, unreasoning, unjustified

00:13:51.449 --> 00:13:56.939
terror which paralyzes needed efforts to convert
retreat into advance.

00:13:56.939 --> 00:14:01.029
After the inaugural address, a woman by the
name Sarah Love said, "Any man who can talk

00:14:01.029 --> 00:14:08.220
like that in times like these is worthy of
every ounce of support a true American has."

00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:12.149
Roosevelt assumed office in the midst of the
greatest financial crisis that the United

00:14:12.149 --> 00:14:14.509
States had ever faced.

00:14:14.509 --> 00:14:19.029
Unemployment was at 25 percent, banks had
closed all over the country and more than

00:14:19.029 --> 00:14:21.589
two million people were homeless.

00:14:21.589 --> 00:14:23.990
Roosevelt dedicated himself to saving the
nation.

00:14:23.990 --> 00:14:27.899
He would do so by putting in place the relief
programs of his first New Deal that were desperately

00:14:27.899 --> 00:14:32.009
needed, restructuring systems that were broken
and invigorating the spirit for his people.

00:14:32.009 --> 00:14:36.339
A key to achieving that invigoration were
his fireside chats, continued from his governorship,

00:14:36.339 --> 00:14:37.339
but now to a national audience.

00:14:37.339 --> 00:14:42.180
He spoke to the American people as a loving
father, full of optimism and good ideas.

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:45.750
During his first term, Roosevelt established
a number of major relief initiatives.

00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:49.509
These included the Federal Relief Administration
and the Public Works Administration, which

00:14:49.509 --> 00:14:53.529
provided employment to a quarter of a million
men throughout the country.

00:14:53.529 --> 00:14:58.810
In August, 1935 he signed the Social Security
Act, which included benefits for the unemployed

00:14:58.810 --> 00:14:59.860
and the elderly.

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:04.680
From 1935, Roosevelt embarked on his second
New Deal, squarely focused on the business

00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:05.680
community.

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:09.720
Wholesale changes were made to the banking
industry and business to make things fairer

00:15:09.720 --> 00:15:11.209
for the everyday man.

00:15:11.209 --> 00:15:16.009
By 1936, Roosevelt's policies and personality
had made him one of the most popular Presidents

00:15:16.009 --> 00:15:17.949
in history.

00:15:17.949 --> 00:15:23.029
The election that year was a landslide victory,
with the president taking every state except

00:15:23.029 --> 00:15:24.779
Maine and Vermont.

00:15:24.779 --> 00:15:28.339
During his second term, Roosevelt spent a
lot of time embroiled in legal challenges

00:15:28.339 --> 00:15:31.310
to many of the programs he had brought in
during his first term.

00:15:31.310 --> 00:15:34.439
In 1936, the Supreme Court was largely conservative.

00:15:34.439 --> 00:15:38.449
However, by 1941, Roosevelt had appointed
seven of the nine justices.

00:15:38.449 --> 00:15:40.769
War
With growing tension between Germany and the

00:15:40.769 --> 00:15:45.140
other European nations, Roosevelt pursued
a policy of US isolationism.

00:15:45.140 --> 00:15:50.220
However, by mid-1938, with public opinion
in the US firmly against German aggression

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:54.129
toward the Jews, he began building up the
military for the possibility of war.

00:15:54.129 --> 00:15:58.720
When the war broke out in September, 1939,
he actively looked for opportunities to support

00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:00.649
Britain and France.

00:16:00.649 --> 00:16:04.040
Roosevelt struck up a great friendship with
Winston Churchill, who became British Prime

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:05.680
Minister in May, 1940.

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:09.399
The United States began supplying an increasing
number of armaments to the British, while,

00:16:09.399 --> 00:16:12.829
at the same time, continuing to build up its
own war footing.

00:16:12.829 --> 00:16:17.190
The Fall of France after just six weeks was
a shock to all of America.

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:21.850
From that point forward, the majority of people
were inclined towards American intervention

00:16:21.850 --> 00:16:23.279
in the European war.

00:16:23.279 --> 00:16:27.499
In 1940, Roosevelt made the decision to run
for an unprecedented 3rd term as President.

00:16:27.499 --> 00:16:31.500
There had been a tradition of limiting Presidency’s
to two terms.

00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:35.810
However, Roosevelt was convinced that a change
of leadership in a time of approaching war

00:16:35.810 --> 00:16:37.749
would be disastrous for the country.

00:16:37.749 --> 00:16:43.579
Throwing himself into the campaign, he won
another overwhelming victory, winning 85%

00:16:43.579 --> 00:16:47.310
of the popular vote and 38 of 48 states.

00:16:47.310 --> 00:16:50.600
Throughout 1941, Roosevelt became overtly
critical of Hitler.

00:16:50.600 --> 00:16:54.569
He became furious when Hitler began threatening
American shipping in the Atlantic, declaring

00:16:54.569 --> 00:16:57.290
. . .
We will not let Hitler prescribe the waters

00:16:57.290 --> 00:17:00.080
of the world on which our ships may travel.

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:04.630
The American flag is not going to be driven
from the seas either by his submarines, his

00:17:04.630 --> 00:17:07.130
airplanes, or his threats.

00:17:07.130 --> 00:17:14.429
On December 7th, 1941, more than 350 Japanese
fighter planes launched a surprise attack

00:17:14.429 --> 00:17:17.439
on the US Naval base at Pearl Harbor, Hawaii.

00:17:17.439 --> 00:17:19.939
Some three thousand people lost their lives.

00:17:19.939 --> 00:17:24.750
Dozens of ships were sunk and many planes
were destroyed as they sat on runways.

00:17:24.750 --> 00:17:28.970
The next day, President Roosevelt addressed
congress with these memorable words . . .

00:17:28.970 --> 00:17:34.231
Yesterday, December 7, 1941, a date which
will live in infamy, the United States of

00:17:34.231 --> 00:17:39.660
America was suddenly and deliberately attacked
by naval and air forces of the Empire of Japan.

00:17:39.660 --> 00:17:43.929
He then asked Congress to declare war on the
Empire of Japan, which it duly did.

00:17:43.929 --> 00:17:47.470
Within days the declaration of war was extended
to include Germany and Italy.

00:17:47.470 --> 00:17:50.910
Franklin Roosevelt was now a wartime President.

00:17:50.910 --> 00:17:54.831
As the US war machine rolled into action with
hundreds of thousands of men being sent to

00:17:54.831 --> 00:17:59.370
the European and Pacific theatres of war,
Roosevelt focused on building up morale on

00:17:59.370 --> 00:18:00.370
the home front.

00:18:00.370 --> 00:18:03.320
His main vehicle for achieving this was still
his fireside chats.

00:18:03.320 --> 00:18:05.860
On February 23rd, 1942 he told the nation
. . .

00:18:05.860 --> 00:18:13.130
Here are three high purposes for every American:
1.

00:18:13.130 --> 00:18:15.970
We shall not stop work for a single day.

00:18:15.970 --> 00:18:25.309
If any dispute arises, we shall keep on working
while the dispute is solved by mediation,

00:18:25.309 --> 00:18:31.690
or conciliation or arbitration—until the
war is won.

00:18:31.690 --> 00:18:32.830
2.

00:18:32.830 --> 00:18:41.090
We shall not demand special gains or special
privileges or special advantages for any one

00:18:41.090 --> 00:18:44.120
group or occupation.

00:18:44.120 --> 00:18:45.360
3.

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:52.740
We shall give up conveniences and modify the
routine of our lives if our country asks us

00:18:52.740 --> 00:18:54.360
to do so.

00:18:54.360 --> 00:19:02.549
We will do it cheerfully, remembering that
the common enemy seeks to destroy every home

00:19:02.549 --> 00:19:06.890
and every freedom in every part of our land.

00:19:06.890 --> 00:19:10.320
With the Americans having just entered the
war, the Russians won a decisive victory in

00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:11.679
the Battle of Stalingrad.

00:19:11.679 --> 00:19:15.610
The Nazis were finally being pushed back toward
Berlin from the east.

00:19:15.610 --> 00:19:18.970
Roosevelt and his generals were convinced
that a similar push from the west would drive

00:19:18.970 --> 00:19:20.490
the Nazis into submission.

00:19:20.490 --> 00:19:23.549
The planning for a grand invasion of Western
Europe began.

00:19:23.549 --> 00:19:28.520
Throughout 1942 and ’43, Roosevelt had a
number of meetings with other allied leaders,

00:19:28.520 --> 00:19:32.320
including Churchill, Josef Stalin and Chinese
leader Chiang-Kai-shek.

00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:37.340
On June 5th, 1944, President Roosevelt addressed
the American people with the following news

00:19:37.340 --> 00:19:40.039
. . .
Yesterday, on June fourth, 1944, Rome fell

00:19:40.039 --> 00:19:42.130
to American and Allied troops.

00:19:42.130 --> 00:19:44.620
The first of the Axis capitals is now in our
hands.

00:19:44.620 --> 00:19:46.940
One up and two to go!

00:19:46.940 --> 00:19:50.840
The following day a major step was taken in
taking out the biggest Axis power.

00:19:50.840 --> 00:19:55.559
On the morning of June 6th, 1944, hundreds
of thousands of allied troops landed on the

00:19:55.559 --> 00:19:56.789
beaches of Normandy.

00:19:56.789 --> 00:19:59.779
After fierce fighting the German army was
pushed back.

00:19:59.779 --> 00:20:02.470
Now the Nazis were being squeezed from both
sides.

00:20:02.470 --> 00:20:05.820
It was now clear to Roosevelt that an Allied
victory was coming.

00:20:05.820 --> 00:20:09.470
He began to place his focus on the logistics
of victory.

00:20:09.470 --> 00:20:12.370
He spoke of the allies as a group of ‘United
Nations.’

00:20:12.370 --> 00:20:17.580
He was convinced that only a world wide union
of nations could prevent future war.

00:20:17.580 --> 00:20:21.490
Roosevelt’s championing of what was to become
the United Nations finally brought into being

00:20:21.490 --> 00:20:29.640
Woodrow Wilson’s dream, which had been imperfectly
realized in the form of the League of Nations.

00:20:29.640 --> 00:20:34.730
With the 1944 Presidential election rapidly
approaching, Roosevelt was felt torn, saying

00:20:34.730 --> 00:20:36.799
. . .
“All that is within me cries out to go back

00:20:36.799 --> 00:20:41.540
to my home on the Hudson River, to avoid public
responsibilities, and to avoid also the publicity

00:20:41.540 --> 00:20:46.250
which in us democracy follows every step of
the Nation’s Chief Executive.

00:20:46.250 --> 00:20:47.909
Such would be my choice.

00:20:47.909 --> 00:20:53.560
But we of this generation chance to live in
a day and hour when our Nation has been attacked,

00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:57.649
and when its future existence and the future
existence of our chosen method of government

00:20:57.649 --> 00:20:59.450
are at stake.”

00:20:59.450 --> 00:21:03.370
Roosevelt ran for a fourth term, but did not
involve himself in the campaign.

00:21:03.370 --> 00:21:04.770
Still, he won again.

00:21:04.770 --> 00:21:09.950
He was now set to serve out sixteen years
as the President of the United States.

00:21:09.950 --> 00:21:15.059
But that wasn’t to be.

00:21:15.059 --> 00:21:17.110
Failing Health

00:21:17.110 --> 00:21:20.309
The stresses of the war had taken a heavy
toll on Roosevelt.

00:21:20.309 --> 00:21:21.860
He was now sixty-two years old.

00:21:21.860 --> 00:21:25.370
He looked weary, suffered from high blood
pressure and a weak heart.

00:21:25.370 --> 00:21:28.640
During the summer of 1944 he came down with
a bad cold.

00:21:28.640 --> 00:21:33.020
He was still sick when he travelled by ship
in February, 1945 to Malta for the ‘Big

00:21:33.020 --> 00:21:34.649
3’ conference with Churchill and Stalin.

00:21:34.649 --> 00:21:35.649
At this conference the division of Germany
was planned out.

00:21:35.649 --> 00:21:39.300
Roosevelt was exhausted when he arrived back
in Washington on February 28th.

00:21:39.300 --> 00:21:41.260
People commented that he looked like he was
dying.

00:21:41.260 --> 00:21:44.100
They were not far wrong.

00:21:44.100 --> 00:21:49.299
Death of a President

00:21:49.299 --> 00:21:51.539
All Roosevelt wanted to do now was to sleep.

00:21:51.539 --> 00:21:55.960
At the beginning of April, he managed to escape
Washington for his retreat at Warm Springs,

00:21:55.960 --> 00:21:56.960
Georgia.

00:21:56.960 --> 00:22:00.450
On the morning of April 12th, he sat while
an artist painted his portrait.

00:22:00.450 --> 00:22:02.480
They stopped for lunch at 1pm.

00:22:02.480 --> 00:22:05.929
Fifteen minutes later, he complained that
he had a terrible headache.

00:22:05.929 --> 00:22:07.649
Then he slumped to the ground.

00:22:07.649 --> 00:22:10.960
He was experiencing a massive cerebral hemorrhage.

00:22:10.960 --> 00:22:16.200
Franklin Delano Roosevelt died at 3:35 pm
on the 12th day of April, 1945.

00:22:16.200 --> 00:22:20.850
It was just 27 days before the war in Europe
came to an end.

00:22:20.850 --> 00:22:26.350
Having guided his country through its worst
depression and its most harrowing war, FDR

00:22:26.350 --> 00:22:27.620
was finally at peace.

00:22:27.620 --> 00:22:33.100
Historians consistently rank Roosevelt, George
Washington, and Abraham Lincoln as the three

00:22:33.100 --> 00:22:34.280
greatest presidents.

00:22:34.280 --> 00:22:39.049
FDR biographer Jean Edward Smith wrote of
Roosevelt, "He lifted himself from a wheelchair

00:22:39.049 --> 00:22:41.200
to lift the nation from its knees."

